WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.010
- In half an hour,

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:04.500
Tower Bridge is going
to open its bascules.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.510
...the bascules are the bit that
the road sits on down below.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:10.290
A sailing barge called Will
wants to pass through the bridge,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.470
and so the bridge must lift.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.370
That's going to delight the tourists,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.211
but it's going to delay thousands of
drivers, cyclists, and pedestrians,

00:00:18.211 --> 00:00:20.810
and cause tailbacks for miles.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.850
And the team here

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:24.230
are going to let me push
the button to do it.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.650
But before we get to that point,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.030
there's a lot of work to be done
by a lot of different people.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.840
- Sailing barge Will, Tower Bridge radio.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.570
- "Tower Bridge radio,
sailing barge Will, good morning."

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:36.440
- Yeah, good morning to you.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.140
Just to let you know
that we are standing by

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.440
for your 10:30 bridge lift.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.580
- We work really closely
with a number of agencies,

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.219
with the Port of London Authority,
responsible for the river,

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:46.700
with Transport for London,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.020
with the police as well,
and also the local boroughs.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.660
The bridge is sat between
two boroughs of London,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.530
half of it's in Tower Hamlets,
and half of it's in Southwark,

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.290
so, in terms of transport network,

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.980
we need really close
working relationships.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.660
If we know that, for example,
there's an ambulance on the way,

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.730
we'll hold off from starting the process.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.880
- Tower Bridge is a tourist
attraction these days.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.610
That's why they've got the
behind-the-scenes tours

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.400
and the glass floors up on the walkways,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.500
but it's still a working bridge,
and it can be opened,

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.790
must legally be opened,

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.770
to let river traffic through
any hour of any day.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:21.650
- There was this one time, back when
Clinton and Blair were in power,

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.730
and I think their timings
ran over slightly,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.550
and the motorcade started to
come back across the bridge,

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.080
from the south side to the north,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.920
and got cut off by a bridge
lift that was due to take place.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.120
Now at that point, the bridge
driver on duty received a call,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.910
"there's a very important person in the car

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.530
"that's stuck on the
south side of the bridge",

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:39.810
Bill Clinton, at the time.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.307
The person in charge said, very bravely,

00:01:42.307 --> 00:01:43.787
"No, we can't do that I'm afraid.

00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:46.347
"The river traffic has
rights over road traffic,

00:01:46.347 --> 00:01:47.757
"but also, physics.

00:01:47.757 --> 00:01:49.297
"There's a boat bearing down on us,

00:01:49.297 --> 00:01:52.010
"if we don't raise the bridge,
it'll strike the bridge."

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.810
So, they raised the
bridge, it came through,

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.750
and they received a lovely
letter from the White House

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.017
a couple of weeks later saying,

00:01:57.017 --> 00:01:59.787
"Thank you for arranging the
brilliant spectacle for us

00:01:59.787 --> 00:02:01.380
"of Tower Bridge raising"!

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:02.880
- When Tower Bridge was built,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.320
cargo was unloaded all around here,
straight into warehouses on the riverbank,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.210
so it was important that ships could
get through the new bridge here.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:15.370
River traffic comes first.
Trade must not be interrupted.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.760
That was laid down in law in 1885 when
the bridge was commissioned, and,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:22.670
even there's no cargo being
unloaded around here these days,

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.250
that law hasn't changed.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.950
Boats have a right to pass through here

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.370
and removing a legal right of way
is very difficult.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.880
There have been a couple
of grumbles about it

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.300
in Parliament over the years,
but as far as I can tell,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.140
no one's ever seriously
thought about changing it.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.010
So, with 15 minutes to go,

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.650
the engineers are getting
ready for the lift.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.800
- We do have pre-checklists

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.160
to make sure that there's no
obstructions in the bascules.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.150
We check the oil levels.
We check the electrics,

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.030
because we've got high voltage
electricity coming in here.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.920
So, when the bridge actually moves,

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.300
it's the initial torque that
you've got to get,

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.620
once it actually starts moving,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.020
it's quite straightforward
and nice and easy.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.970
We've got four packs on
each corner of the bridge,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.850
and one hydraulic pack has
two hydraulic motors

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.840
with two hydraulic pumps.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.460
Back in the day, up until the early '70s,
it was steam operated.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.440
What we actually use today

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.510
is very similar to what the
Victorians actually put in,

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.290
and these days, it's just
the electric hydraulics.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:22.720
- If you've got a tall enough boat,

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.220
then you can call Tower Bridge
24 hours ahead,

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.000
ask for a bridge lift,
and they will fit you in.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.570
Even though you can't really get very far.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.859
The next bridge is only about 900 metres
down the river, and it doesn't move.

00:03:32.859 --> 00:03:35.680
And never mind that lifting Tower
Bridge causes tailbacks and

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.720
massively inconveniences a lot of people!

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.230
If your boat's tall enough,

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.750
then you've got a right to
get the bridge lifted,

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.660
and about five minutes' time,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.420
someone's going to use that right.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.911
- Official numbers are six bridge drivers,

00:03:49.911 --> 00:03:52.700
and so there's only six of us in the world
that can actually do it.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.690
It takes, on average, about two years
to train up to be a bridge driver.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:57.540
The main part of being a bridge driver

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.400
is not actually raising
and lowering the bridge,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.460
it's knowing what to do if it goes wrong.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.100
If you've got the bridge up in
the air, and it breaks down,

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:07.920
it's a matter of: you've got to be able
to fix it as quick as possible,

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.080
so that you're not
blocking up half of London.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.530
[announcing] "Bridge driving control systems
about to be switched on.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:17.540
"Stand clear of moving structures,
machinery, and controls."

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:18.373
- All right.

00:04:18.373 --> 00:04:19.650
- Hi, Tom.
- Hello!

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.940
- Come on in.
- Thank you!

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:25.670
- Welcome to the northeast control cabin.
- Thank you very much.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:27.230
- As you know, we're
about to do a bridge lift.

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:28.110
- Yep.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:29.500
- It's going to be for
the Sailing Barge Will,

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.930
which is just down the
river, just down there.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:34.570
- All right.
- Time is now...

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:36.750
- 10:26.
- Yeah.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.280
- Four minutes.
- So we're going to go straight into this.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.230
- Let's do it.
- No time to waste.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.779
- I'm actually nervous.

00:04:44.104 --> 00:04:46.430
- So the first thing I want you to do
is start the power hydraulic pumps.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:50.370
- Okay.
- So, press that button.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.730
That will now start the pumps.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.622
"This is a public announcement.

00:04:54.622 --> 00:04:56.970
"Bridge lifting operations
are about to commence.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.460
"Stand by bridge staff,
stopping road traffic."

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:03.720
If you could just press
the red traffic light?

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.306
- Traffic lights changing to red.
[brakes squeal]

00:05:05.306 --> 00:05:06.139
- That's it, let go.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:09.230
So, now we can press them
together, at this juncture.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.020
- Done.
- Now we'll press for the pedestrian gates.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:12.853
- All right.

00:05:12.853 --> 00:05:14.030
Closed?
- Yep.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:18.888
- Just got to wait for two casually
sauntering tourists going across there.

00:05:18.888 --> 00:05:20.280
- Yeah, this is why we
start a little bit earlier.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.980
- Are you ever tempted to just
start it raising at this point?

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.014
- [laughs] No.

00:05:24.014 --> 00:05:25.260
Press that now.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:26.950
- All right, that's disengaging.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:30.070
- Yep, so the indication will show...
- That light's gone off.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.897
I'm terrified I've pushed
a wrong button already.

00:05:31.897 --> 00:05:34.233
- No, no, you've done nothing wrong.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.550
Next one is the nose bolts.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.090
- Retract the nose bolts?
- Yep, press that button.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.530
- I feel like I should
hold it down for longer,

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:41.370
but it's just a quick tap.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.910
- It's just a quick tap.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.070
- Bridge ready to move.
- Yep.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.653
- Grab hold of the joystick,
pull it back slightly,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.610
- Whoo!
- Little bit more.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:54.026
- All the way? All the way.

00:05:54.026 --> 00:05:55.937
- That's it, all the way.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:58.870
- Tell me...
- You're now raisin' the bridge.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:01.560
- Yep, tell me when to slow it down.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.310
- It's going to be a matter of
once we get to the angle,

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:04.810
you just let go of the joystick.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.780
- Now, to me, from here,
that looks like 45 already,

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.230
but we're only at 26.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.020
- That's right.
- It's deceiving.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.517
- The way to see, at the
corner of the bridge there,

00:06:13.517 --> 00:06:17.983
you see that bit of iron with riveting?
- Yeah.

00:06:17.983 --> 00:06:21.209
- When that gets horizontal,
that's actually 45 degrees.

00:06:21.209 --> 00:06:22.130
- And here comes the barge.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.787
That is much taller than
it looked from a distance.

00:06:24.787 --> 00:06:25.800
- It does.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.970
- 39 degrees and 41.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.713
- Right, so if you let go of it now,

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:36.130
and press the green river light...
that's it.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.120
- And Sailing Barge Will, is good to go.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.570
- Yep.
- It's a shame I'm wearing the mask,

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:43.307
because you can't see the
grin on my face right now.

00:06:43.307 --> 00:06:47.500
- It's a great feeling when you
do it for the first time.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:48.390
- Yeah.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:49.740
Thank you so much!

00:06:51.038 --> 00:06:53.946
- You have completed a bridge lift.

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:55.730
- That was much more nerve-racking,

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:58.340
than just pushing some buttons
should have been.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.550
There's so much riding on that.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.630
I know it's all automated.
I know that, at any point,

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.840
you could have pushed me out of the way
and stepped in and that's fine,

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.110
but it's still nerve-racking when
there are so many people waiting

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:13.670
and so much riding on that
for, you know...London working.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:18.237
- But even now, I've done
hundreds of lifts,

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.435
and I still get a buzz out of it.

00:07:20.435 --> 00:07:21.730
- Yeah!

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.083
Right, how do we get this back down?

